 "question": "After spinal anesthesia, do heart rate and the pulse-oximeter waveform improve the forecast of the next cuff reading (MAP) beyond the cuff readings alone?",
 "answer_line": "No. In 200 non-cesarean spinal cases (cross-validated), heart rate and the pleth waveform did not meet the pre-specified rule (−0.1%, from 8.67 to 8.68 mmHg; rule: at least 5%, with the 95% CI excluding zero). Gains larger than 0.2% (0.01 mmHg) are not compatible with these data (approximate 95% CI), for this feature set and a linear model.",
 "sentence_1": "In 200 non-cesarean spinal cases (cross-validated), heart rate and pleth waveform features did not meet the pre-specified rule for lowering next-reading MAP forecast error beyond a cuff-only third-order model (−0.1%, Δ 0.01 mmHg, 95% CI −0.01 to 0.03; rule: at least 5%, with the 95% CI excluding zero). Gains larger than 0.2% (0.01 mmHg) are not compatible with these data (approximate 95% CI, see §6.2 of SPEC), for this feature set and a linear model.",
 "sentence_3": "In the 30 fast-cycling development cases (median cuff interval < 90 s): 0.3% (Δ −0.03 mmHg, 95% CI −0.07 to 0.01); one extra cuff reading's value recovered by the added signals: −0.9% (95% CI −2.2% to −0.1%) (not distinguishable from placebo inputs)."
